  "text": "Question three. What is the difference between OLTP and oh-lap systems? OLTP, online transaction processing, serves many small reads and writes of single rows, with strict consistency: an app's Postgres, stored by row. Oh-lap, online analytical processing, serves a few large scans and aggregations: the warehouse, stored by column. So data is extracted from the first, loaded into the second, and transformed there. That last step is the analytics engineer's. Run heavy analytics on the app's own database instead, and it competes with the app, and runs badly.\n\nQuestion four. What is columnar storage, and why does it matter for analytics? It keeps each column's values together on disk, instead of each row's. An analytical query touches a few columns across many rows, so it reads only those columns. And values of one type, side by side, compress far better. A million region names take five and a half megabytes as text, and three hundred eighty kilobytes as one encoded column. Row stores still win at reading whole rows one at a time. Column stores win at aggregating billions of them.",
